WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.210
A song you have loved for eleven years is playing, and a piece of news arrives about the person singing it.

2
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:12.900
The chorus comes around — the same chorus, the same frequencies, nothing in the air has changed.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.390
So what exactly broke: the note, or the ear?

4
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:32.720
It is getting close to midnight, you are washing dishes in the kitchen, and in your headphones a song you have loved for eleven years is playing.

5
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.130
You met a summer through that song, you went through a breakup with it, you played it for a friend.

6
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.140
Then your phone buzzes.

7
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.870
A news story.

8
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.840
About the person singing the song, something serious.

9
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.850
The song keeps playing while you read.

10
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.060
The chorus comes around.

11
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.230
The same chorus.

12
00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:01.120
Something very strange is happening here, and we usually pass over it without stopping.

13
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.450
Nothing in the air has changed.

14
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:12.740
The frequency of the sound waves is the same, the melody walks the same path, that second voice you love enters at exactly the same moment.

15
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.070
Physically, nothing in the world has happened.

16
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.600
But something has happened.

17
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.290
The chorus now grates on you.

18
00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:27.020
Your finger moves to those three little dots on the screen, and they say: remove from playlist.

19
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.630
The question is this: what spoiled?

20
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:33.680
The note, or the ear?

21
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.370
When this question comes up, the first reaction is always the same: you have to separate the work from the artist.

22
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:44.020
It sounds mature, cool-headed and fair.

23
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.310
And besides, we all already do it.

24
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:54.040
Carlo Gesualdo, prince of Venosa, killed his wife and her lover in fifteen ninety.

25
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.850
He never stood trial, because he was a nobleman.

26
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:07.580
The madrigals he wrote are still sung today, still counted among the boldest harmonic discoveries in the history of music, still silencing concert halls with a shiver.

27
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.630
Nobody walks out.

28
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:18.400
Caravaggio killed a man in Rome in sixteen oh six, fled the city with a death sentence hanging over his head, and went on painting as a fugitive.

29
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.530
His paintings still hang in churches, directly above the altar.

30
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.860
Nobody in the church feels uneasy.

31
00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.350
So we do know how to separate the work from its maker.

32
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.320
But notice: we do it not as a skill but as distance.

33
00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:39.050
We did not forgive Gesualdo, we simply do not live on the same street as him.

34
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.340
The name of the woman he killed is a footnote to us.

35
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.990
Four hundred years did the separating on our behalf.

36
00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.320
Part of what we take for maturity is actually time.

37
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:58.770
That is why the principle of separation reaches four centuries back without effort, yet falls apart in our hands when it comes to last night's news.

38
00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:06.580
Because as we get closer we start laying two different questions on top of one another, and the moment they blur, the argument is poisoned.

39
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.390
The first question is this: is this a good work?

40
00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.840
The second is this: can I keep up my relationship with this work?

41
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.770
The first is a judgment about the work, the second is a decision about you.

42
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.780
And the two are not the same thing.

43
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:27.390
Because the human mind loads objects with the trace of whoever has touched them.

44
00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:31.520
In psychology it is called contamination.

45
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:42.370
The experiments of Paul Rozin and his colleagues showed something simple: people do not want to wear a sweater that has been worn by someone they dislike, someone they find morally disgusting.

46
00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:46.020
They do not want it even once the sweater has been washed.

47
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.990
Washing reduces the reluctance but does not zero it out.

48
00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.360
There is nothing in the sweater.

49
00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.010
The cotton is there, the thread is there.

50
00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.380
The trace is in us.

51
00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.910
A song is a sweater in this sense.

52
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:07.760
While you were listening you opened yourself to it, you let someone in, you carried their voice around inside your head.

53
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.370
What spoiled when the news arrived was not the note, it was that openness.

54
00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:20.820
And admitting this is a more honest starting point than repeating the sentence you have to separate the work, because it finally makes visible why separating is so hard.

55
00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.430
So did philosophy meet this question for the first time yesterday?

56
00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.320
No.

57
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:33.370
It has two great answers, exact opposites of each other, and both are surprisingly radical.

58
00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:39.580
In the third book of the Republic, Plato describes a poet arriving at the ideal city he has built.

59
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:46.750
A master poet, able to imitate anything, produce any sound, bring any emotion to life, comes to the gate.

60
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.360
Plato does not beat him, humiliate him or throw him in prison.

61
00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:58.850
Quite the opposite: he pours oil on his head, crowns him with a garland, and respectfully sends him off to another city.

62
00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.500
The politest expulsion in the history of philosophy.

63
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.150
He walks him to the door and sends him on his way.

64
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.400
Why?

65
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.890
Notice, not because the poems are bad.

66
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.860
Plato's problem is the reverse: because they are too good.

67
00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.590
What he says in the tenth book is this: the one who imitates does not know the thing he imitates.

68
00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:29.840
The painter draws a horse's bridle but never learned from the harness maker what a bridle is for; the poet tells of heroism but does not know how to command a war.

69
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.010
The work is brilliant, the maker is ignorant.

70
00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:40.980
And brilliance is dangerous precisely because it carries that ignorance, because it enters the soul directly, without asking reason's permission.

71
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:46.390
So Plato's criterion is, directly, the maker's relationship with truth.

72
00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:49.560
He looks at the maker in order to judge the work.

73
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:58.170
Anyone who says today that they cannot listen without thinking about the artist is standing, without realizing it, in a camp two thousand four hundred years old.

74
00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.820
Now cross over to the opposite camp.

75
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:14.110
In nineteen forty-six two American critics, Wimsatt and Beardsley, described what they called the intentional fallacy: what the poet wanted to do neither can be nor should be used to measure what the poem is.

76
00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:20.320
Even if the poet explains his intention, that explanation is a new text, not the poem itself.

77
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:26.530
The moment the poem is finished it has left the poet's hands; it is public, it is in the language.

78
00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:32.260
In nineteen sixty-seven Roland Barthes said this far more harshly, and killed the author outright.

79
00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:43.270
In the short essay he titled The Death of the Author, what he describes is this: a text is not an original message gushing out of a single person, it is a fabric woven from countless citations;

80
00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.400
meaning is born not where it is written but where it is read.

81
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:56.330
To place the author behind the text and say, look, this is what he really meant, is to close the text, to sentence it to a single meaning.

82
00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:00.140
For Barthes, when the author dies the reader is born.

83
00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:05.230
In nineteen sixty-nine Foucault entered the same place from somewhere entirely different.

84
00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:09.680
He did not ask who the author is, he asked what the author's name does.

85
00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:19.570
His answer was this: a name is a classifying device that groups texts, assumes consistency among them, and determines which of them is to be taken seriously.

86
00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:23.860
The author is not the source of the text but a border drawn around it.

87
00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.150
He opened his essay with the question, what does it matter who is speaking.

88
00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.160
Two answers, two extremes.

89
00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:37.370
Plato says you cannot separate the work from its maker, because the work carries the maker's ignorance.

90
00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:43.260
Barthes says you cannot bind the work to its maker, because the work no longer belongs to the maker.

91
00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.190
So what happens when these two extremes are put to the test in the middle of life?

92
00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:53.920
In eighteen fifty Richard Wagner published a text under a pen name: Jewishness in Music.

93
00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.570
It argued that music had been corrupted by a people.

94
00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:06.660
Seventy years later his music, long after his own death, became the ceremonial music of the Nazi regime; the regime used him like a flag.

95
00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:13.350
From nineteen thirty-eight onward, orchestras in Palestine, and later in Israel, stopped playing Wagner.

96
00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:19.240
It was never banned by law, nobody took a written decision, but for seventy years nobody played it.

97
00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:24.010
In July two thousand one Daniel Barenboim gave a concert in Jerusalem.

98
00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:26.380
The program had been changed.

99
00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:35.630
He was originally going to play Wagner, and after objections from camp survivors and discomfort from the government he changed the program and played Schumann and Stravinsky.

100
00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:43.120
At the end of the concert he turned back to the hall and offered to play the prelude to Wagner's opera Tristan und Isolde as an encore.

101
00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:47.250
And he did not simply play it, he opened a discussion.

102
00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:53.940
In a hall holding roughly two thousand eight hundred people, from the stage, arguments were made for and against.

103
00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:59.670
In the end he said this: I am going to play it, and anyone who is uncomfortable may leave.

104
00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.720
Some left.

105
00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.210
The rest listened, and gave a standing ovation.

106
00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:09.660
In that hall both philosophical answers collapse.

107
00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:18.270
Plato's answer, look at the maker, silences that music completely; you bury one of the most fragile beauties in the world because of a pamphlet.

108
00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:30.240
Barthes's answer, the author is dead, cannot be said to the face of a person sitting in that hall whose grandfather's name appears on the lists; telling him this music has nothing to do with anyone is an insult.

109
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.570
What Barenboim did was not to choose a theory.

110
00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:36.420
It was to ask the people in the room.

111
00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:42.950
And this shows us the real thing: this is not a question of rules, it is a question of the room.

112
00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.440
The answer changes according to who is in the room.

113
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:54.730
That is an uncomfortable conclusion, because everybody here wants a principle, a criterion they can put in their pocket and apply to every situation.

114
00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.900
Is there such a criterion?

115
00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.670
Another tradition has a remarkably clear distinction on exactly this point.

116
00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:05.840
Ninth-century Baghdad.

117
00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:15.250
Al-Kindi, the first great philosopher of the Islamic world, writes in his book on first philosophy that we should not be ashamed to accept the truth from wherever it comes,

118
00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:24.340
to take it even if it comes from distant nations and foreign peoples; that for the seeker of truth nothing is more valuable than truth, and that what is shameful is to belittle it.

119
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:34.390
He writes this at a moment when Greek philosophy is only just being translated into Arabic, when it would have been very easy to reject this knowledge as the knowledge of those who are not ours.

120
00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:42.840
There is a saying settled deep into the language of that same tradition: wisdom is the believer's lost property, and wherever he finds it he takes it.

121
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:52.250
Scholars of hadith consider the chain of transmission of this saying weak, meaning its attribution to the Prophet is disputed; but the meaning has settled in as a principle.

122
00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:59.740
Alongside it circulates a sharper sentence attributed to Ali: truth is not known through men, men are known through truth.

123
00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:07.710
In other words, if you are trying to deduce the correctness of a statement from the standing of whoever said it, you are holding your scales upside down.

124
00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:11.840
Al-Ghazali pointed to the error at the very other end of this matter.

125
00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:19.010
He is the man who settled accounts with the philosophers most harshly; he tried to refute their metaphysics line by line.

126
00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:27.780
But in the book where he tells the story of his own life, while listing the sciences of the philosophers, he set mathematics and logic apart and said this:

127
00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:38.470
if someone finds the truths of geometry in a philosopher's book and, out of his anger at the philosopher, rejects those truths along with him, he believes he is serving religion but in fact harms it.

128
00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:46.600
Because a person who denies something plainly true for the sake of his faith has announced to the one across from him that his faith cannot make peace with the truth.

129
00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:55.850
So al-Ghazali's warning is about the price of throwing out the work together with its maker, and that warning is made by the very man who criticized those makers most.

130
00:11:55.850 --> 00:12:07.340
Ibn Rushd said the same principle in the language of a jurist: if someone who came before us has spoken the truth, we take it with gratitude, and which faith he belonged to does not change that exchange.

131
00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:12.910
Now let us come to the finest point of this tradition, because the real criterion is there.

132
00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:19.920
The same tradition that says take wisdom wherever you find it also built the most detailed system of source scrutiny in history.

133
00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:30.290
In the science of hadith there is a branch called ilm al-rijal: the biographies of those who transmit reports, their memories, their honesty, from whom they heard and to whom they passed it on.

134
00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:36.020
Al-jarh wa al-ta'dil, the weighing of whether a transmitter is to be counted trustworthy.

135
00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:42.070
The names, the characters, the strength of memory of thousands of people were put on record.

136
00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.920
Here, who is speaking is everything.

137
00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:47.290
This is not a contradiction.

138
00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:50.300
It is a precise distinction.

139
00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.270
Because the two are talking about two different things.

140
00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:58.400
The first is burhan, that is, demonstrative proof.

141
00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:03.810
The man who says two times two is four may be a liar, a tyrant or a murderer.

142
00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:06.500
The result is four.

143
00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:13.190
Because proof carries its own evidence inside itself; you can erase the name on the paper and check it from the beginning.

144
00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:17.160
Proof has no source, proof has steps.

145
00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.650
The second is khabar, that is, testimony.

146
00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:25.900
In the words of someone who says I saw it, there is nothing to be checked from the inside.

147
00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:29.070
The only thing you have is that person.

148
00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:37.200
In testimony, judging the source is not a right but a necessity, because if you separate the testimony from the witness nothing is left standing.

149
00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.570
There is the criterion.

150
00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:44.820
Who is speaking gains its weight according to whether the thing you are taking is proof or testimony.

151
00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.190
So which is art?

152
00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:53.080
And here we have to face an uncomfortable answer: art is neither, but it stands closer to testimony.

153
00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:57.370
A novel proves nothing; you cannot check it step by step.

154
00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:01.500
What it does is this: it says, look at the world from here.

155
00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:08.670
It shows how a person is loved, how a pain is carried, who is worth taking seriously.

156
00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:14.080
It offers you a gaze, and it asks a trust of you so that you will adopt that gaze.

157
00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:19.010
Wherever trust is asked for, who is asking makes the question legitimate.

158
00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:25.220
This is why a mathematics book written by a murderer and a love story written by a murderer are not the same thing.

159
00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.870
From one you take the formula and leave the man behind.

160
00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:37.800
In the other, what you are taking is not a formula but a gaze, and knowing what the owner of that gaze did with the very same gaze becomes part of reading the text.

161
00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:47.530
In the last year of his life, in sixteen fifty-six, Kâtip Çelebi wrote the Mîzânü'l-hak, a name meaning the balance of truth in choosing what is most correct.

162
00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:59.340
Throughout the book he enters the quarrels of his age and, interestingly, criticizes both sides: those who reject knowledge as irreligion, and those who defend the matter without thinking about it at all.

163
00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:04.270
His scale is always the same: look not at the camp, but at the matter itself.

164
00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:10.480
The thing is, the scale alone is not enough, because in life this question also comes with a bill.

165
00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:18.130
In practice there are two things people do, and the two are often taken for the same thing: boycotting, and separating.

166
00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:24.340
In fact three different things are tangled together, and the moment you untangle them half the argument goes out.

167
00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:28.470
The first is aesthetic judgment: is this work well made?

168
00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:32.440
The second is personal relationship: can I listen to this?

169
00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:39.930
The third is material support: when I watch this, when I listen to it, when I buy the ticket, who am I giving the money to?

170
00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:43.420
Notice, only the third of these is a real lever.

171
00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:49.790
You cannot change the aesthetic judgment; you cannot forget that something well made is well made simply by wishing to.

172
00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:54.880
You cannot force the personal relationship; if it grates, it grates.

173
00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:57.410
But you can direct the money.

174
00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:01.220
And right here things do not turn out the way you think.

175
00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:06.470
Listening to Gesualdo earns nobody a penny; there is no one there left to be paid.

176
00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:11.560
With the living, the money you pay most often does not go where you imagine it goes.

177
00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:21.610
The biggest music streaming services say they pass on roughly seventy percent of their revenue to rights holders; but the rights holder is often not the artist but a company that has bought the catalogue.

178
00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:31.500
When you boycott a film, where you think the number of people you are punishing is three or five, it is three hundred people whose names you have never heard: the lighting technician, the editor,

179
00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:36.430
the woman who wrote the music, the costume assistant.

180
00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:45.520
Your staying away is not a verdict, it is an arithmetic, and if you do not know who the arithmetic lands on, believing you have passed judgment is fooling yourself.

181
00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:55.410
People actually found intuitively what can genuinely be separated out, and what they have done in recent years was exactly about that: separating the name from the object.

182
00:16:55.410 --> 00:17:04.500
In the summer of two thousand nineteen the Louvre in Paris, after protests, covered over the Sackler family's name on the walls of one of its wings;

183
00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:11.670
the museum's management recalled that the naming rights were limited to twenty years and that the donation had been made in nineteen ninety-seven.

184
00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:20.280
In December two thousand twenty-one the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York, the Met, removed the same name from seven of its galleries.

185
00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:23.290
Notice: nobody touched the works.

186
00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:28.380
The Temple of Dendur stayed where it stood, not a single display case was emptied.

187
00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:32.030
What came down was honor; what remained was the work.

188
00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:36.000
Because standing can be taken back, the work cannot.

189
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:43.170
You can pull a name off a wall; you cannot pull out and throw away what a sentence written a hundred years ago did to you.

190
00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:50.820
Most of what people do under the name of boycott is in fact this: not destroying the work, but withdrawing the honor given to it.

191
00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:55.750
And separating these two from one another changes the way you see the whole argument.

192
00:17:55.750 --> 00:18:03.080
But here there is the reverse question of this entire subject, the one nobody asks, and that is the genuinely hard one.

193
00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:06.890
We always ask whether the maker contaminates the work.

194
00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.340
Yet sometimes the work gives the maker away.

195
00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:16.430
Heidegger joined the party in nineteen thirty-three and became rector at Freiburg.

196
00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:24.400
For decades the debate ran in this form: does a thinker's political choice invalidate his philosophy, or do the texts stand apart?

197
00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:35.890
From two thousand fourteen onward, once the Black Notebooks were published, the debate changed, because the issue was no longer the man's biography; the flaw itself was speaking from inside the texts.

198
00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:44.180
There, the move of separating the work fails not for moral reasons but for philological ones: there is no place left at which to separate.

199
00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:47.670
This is the criterion we were looking for.

200
00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:50.680
Not a rule, but a reading.

201
00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:56.570
The question is not whether the maker was bad, but whether the flaw is inside the work or beside it.

202
00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.580
There is no murder in Caravaggio's painting.

203
00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:03.870
There is no blood of jealousy in Gesualdo's madrigal.

204
00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:12.800
If a text contains the refusal to count a human being as human, that text is no longer the maker's private life, it is the text's own claim.

205
00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:18.050
The first you can separate; if you separate the second, it means you are not reading.

206
00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:22.980
And today this question weighs more than it used to, because the distance is gone.

207
00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:29.990
In the seventeenth century a work reached you already purified of its maker; you would see the painting, you would not know the man.

208
00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:36.360
Now every maker's face, voice, yesterday's sentence, quarrel and court case stands in front of it.

209
00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:40.490
The default condition is no longer separation but entanglement.

210
00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:44.780
And so the skill to be developed is not purification.

211
00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:53.390
Purification means this: cleaning out the list, emptying the shelf, arranging everything around you so that you will never be uneasy again.

212
00:19:53.390 --> 00:20:01.200
Its opposite extreme is just as easy: saying nothing matters, placing aesthetics above everything and refusing to see what happened.

213
00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:05.970
Both exits rescue a person from the same thing: from carrying the weight.

214
00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:11.860
Carrying is this: being able to hold two things at once in the same sentence.

215
00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:13.590
This is well made.

216
00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:16.120
The person who made it did that.

217
00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:20.250
And neither of those two sentences cancels the other.

218
00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:26.300
When you say the first you are not absolving the second; when you say the second you are not erasing the first.

219
00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:33.150
The human mind does not like this; it wants consistency, it wants a single emotion, it wants all or nothing.

220
00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:38.400
Most of what we call maturity is learning not to resolve this discomfort but to carry it.

221
00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:44.130
And there is one final asymmetry, and it explains why this whole matter never closes.

222
00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:51.300
To judge a person you have to know them: what they did knowingly, what they were forced into, what they came to regret.

223
00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:58.150
To judge a work, looking at it is enough; it stands there and lays out all of its evidence in front of you.

224
00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:02.120
Of these two we only ever hold one.

225
00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.290
The work is in front of us, the maker is not.

226
00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:09.900
That is why judging the work is easy and judging the maker is cheap.

227
00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:16.750
And most of the time, when we delete something from the playlist, we do not know ourselves which of the two we are deciding.

228
00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:21.200
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

229
00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:24.530
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
